the historic heat that jeff was talking  about in the west is blamed for what may  be  record-breaking hospitalizations in  washington state  hospitals report nearly 1800 emergency  room visits for heat-related illness  in this past week at least 100 people  have died in the west and that includes  a farm worker in oregon  the punishing heat is bad news for all  farm workers in that region  lilia luciano looks at efforts to  protect them  for nearly a week farm workers at this  southern washington state cherry farm  have faced temperatures as high  as 113 degrees this is a crisis  it's an emergency we followed volunteers  with the united farm workers union  as they handed out cold drinks  and explained the signs of heat  exhaustion and heat stroke  jolanda carrion told us farmworkers who  are typically paid by the amount of  crops they pick  are often hesitant to take breaks what  does it feel like  you start feeling nauseous this heat  wave has been different  it's been a lot harder many including  this 11 year old named juan  start their shift as early as 3 a.m to  avoid the heat  and until what time are you working  i don't really know now the united farm  workers union is pushing for safeguards  like those already in place in  california  we are demanding that the governor  implement emergency rules for this heat  crisis for our farm workers here in  washington state  they need cold water they need rest  breaks and they need  shade in the fields  volunteers also honored farm workers who  have lost their lives  including sebastian francisco perez an  oregon farm worker from guatemala who  died last weekend  his death raises concerns about the heat  in the weeks and months ahead  one thing that worries me the most is  that there's children out here working  and that a lot of the workers don't feel  comfortable calling 9-1-1  that was lilia luciano reporting from  washington state
 

